It is also sometimes convenient to write short methods just inside foreign_class!, for example to add a way to access fields of a struct or to export a result of a Rust macro call: foreign_class!(class TestFnInline { fn int_to_str(a: i32) -> String { format!("{}", a) }
}); To reference self in inline methods you should use the this variable, because the inline method is not the real one, it is just code block that gets included into the generated code: pub struct Session { name: String,
} foreign_class!( #[derive(SmartPtrCopy)] class Session { self_type Session; constructor session_init(name: &str) -> Rc<RefCell<Session>> { Rc::new(RefCell::new(Session { name: name.into(), })) } fn name(&self) -> &str { &this.name } }
);

