You can use #[derive(SmartPtrCopy)] when a foreign class's constructor returns Rc<...> or Arc<...>. Flapigen clones the smart pointer when another owner needs the value; it does not clone the Rust object inside it. In C++, it generates a copy constructor and copy assignment operator. The C++ example has a counter backed by Rc<RefCell<_>>: pub struct SharedCounter { value: i32,
} impl SharedCounter { fn new() -> std::rc::Rc<std::cell::RefCell<Self>> { std::rc::Rc::new(std::cell::RefCell::new(Self { value: 0 })) } fn increment(&mut self) { self.value += 1; } fn value(&self) -> i32 { self.value }
} foreign_class!( #[derive(SmartPtrCopy)] class SharedCounter { self_type SharedCounter; constructor SharedCounter::new() -> Rc<RefCell<SharedCounter>>; fn SharedCounter::increment(&mut self); fn SharedCounter::value(&self) -> i32; }
); SharedCounter counter; SharedCounter second_handle = counter; // clones Rc, not SharedCounter second_handle.increment(); SharedCounter assigned; assigned = counter; assigned.increment(); if (counter.value() != 2 || second_handle.value() != 2) { return EXIT_FAILURE; } counter, second_handle, and assigned are three C++ handles to the same Rust counter. Incrementing through either copy changes the value read through counter. In Java, passing a wrapper by value to Rust clones its smart pointer, so the Java wrapper remains usable and Rust can retain the shared value. The Android example stores an Arc<Mutex<Session>> in another Rust object: struct SessionStore { session: Arc<Mutex<Session>>,
} impl SessionStore { fn new(session: Arc<Mutex<Session>>) -> Self { Self { session } } fn saved_base(&self) -> i32 { self.session.lock().unwrap().a }
} foreign_class!(#[derive(SmartPtrCopy)] class Session { self_type Session; constructor Session::new() -> Arc<Mutex<Session>>; fn Session::add_and1(&self, val: i32) -> i32; fn Session::set_base(&mut self, base: i32); fn Session::greet(to: &str) -> String;
}); foreign_class!(class SessionStore { self_type SessionStore; constructor SessionStore::new(session: Arc<Mutex<Session>>) -> SessionStore; fn SessionStore::saved_base(&self) -> i32;
}); Session session = MyApplication.get().getSession(); final int res = session.add_and1(2); SessionStore store = new SessionStore(session); // Rust retains an Arc clone session.set_base(7); // the Java wrapper remains usable final int sharedBase = store.saved_base(); tv_number.setText(getString(R.string.rust_results_text, res, sharedBase)); store.delete(); The store observes changes made through the original Session. This derive does not add a Java copy constructor; its effect is on ownership when the wrapper is passed to Rust.

