For a regular foreign_class!, Rust &Foo arguments and return values use FooRef by value in C++. A Foo or const Foo& converts implicitly to FooRef, so either an owning object or an existing borrowed view can be passed to a generated method. For example, the C++ test fixture uses TestReferences and Foo: TestReferences source{1, "source"};
TestReferences target{2, "target"};
FooRef view = source.get_foo_ref();
target.update_foo(view);
const Foo owned{3, "owned"};
target.update_foo(owned); Copying a FooRef copies only its pointer. Keep the Rust object alive while using the view. Rust &mut Foo arguments still take Foo& in C++; a returned &mut Foo currently becomes a read-only FooRef.

